Package: ivate
Title: Informative Inference on the Average Treatment Effect with Instrumental Variables
Version: 0.1.0
Authors@R:
    person("Luca", "Locher", email = "luca.locher@uzh.ch", role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Provides tools for sensitivity analysis and partial identification
    of the average treatment effect (ATE) in instrumental variable (IV)
    settings with a binary instrument and a binary treatment. Under the
    standard IV assumptions of Angrist, Imbens, and Rubin (1996)
    <doi:10.1080/01621459.1996.10476902>, point identification of the ATE is
    precluded by two unobserved quantities: the mean potential outcome under
    control for always-takers and the mean potential outcome under treatment
    for never-takers. This package derives the thresholds on these
    sensitivity parameters at which the ATE equals a policy-relevant decision
    threshold, expresses them as interpretable implied comparisons, and
    computes bounds on the ATE under user-supplied restrictions. Uncertainty
    is quantified by the nonparametric bootstrap or, for binary outcomes, by
    Bayesian inference based on the transparent parametrization of
    Richardson, Evans, and Robins (2011), which also yields posterior
    distributions of the ATE as a function of, or under priors on, the
    sensitivity parameters. Both one-sided and two-sided non-compliance are
    handled automatically.
